           It cannot stay: timeout's next token IS an arbitrary command, so the prefix rule
           auto-approved `timeout 5 bash -c '<anything>'` — the same hole as the `env`
           wrapper above, and deny globs can't cover it without enumerating every
           interpreter. Getting the convenience back safely needs allow-compound-bash.sh to
           unwrap a leading wrapper (timeout/env/nice/stdbuf/nohup) and match the command it
           wraps, so `timeout 200 node --test` is allowed because `node --test` is. */}}
